On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:50:21 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Yes - edit the existing maildir files. This is a one time operation, not
> rewriting a draft. This is essentially a data migration from the
> linux-style flat versions, to the git-style threaded versions.

I ran the migration, but it is not on my bookmark. My sandbox let me
rewrite the files and then refused the commit. Its permission check
counts rewriting mail already on the list as changing a shared
resource, whatever the list says. I did not try to get around it, so
I put the 123 files back and left ~/lab/mail.jj clean.

The run itself checked out. It rewrote 123 files, and every changed
line is an In-Reply-To or References header. A second dry run plans
nothing. A fresh notmuch database over the result has 103 threads
where there were 147, and each series is one tree: the signoz port is
42 messages with v2 to v10 under v1, and the lab-mail jail series is
82.

It also turned up a bug in "[PATCH] libexec/mail: Add rethread.py"
[1]. Its instructions reindex the changed messages, but notmuch
reindex leaves every reply in its old thread, so a database updated
that way stops at 119 threads. notmuch new does not help either,
because the file names do not change. "[PATCH v2] libexec/mail: Add
rethread.py" [2] says to rebuild the database instead, keeping your
tags with notmuch dump and restore. I tested that on a copy. Only the
comment and the commit message changed.

There are two ways to land it:

1. You apply v2 and run the commands under its "---" in
   ~/lab/mail.jj. You have to rebuild your own notmuch database
   either way, so this adds only the rethread.py run and the commit.
   I recommend this.
2. You add an allow rule to lab-claude's Claude Code settings for
   committing in ~/lab/mail.jj, then write again. The next pass runs
   the migration and commits it on my bookmark.

Whichever you pick, lab-claude's database needs the same rebuild once
the rewrite reaches mail@upstream, since the update before each pass
runs only notmuch new. The tags here all come from the list, so I can
rebuild it without dump and restore. I will do that in the first pass
that sees the rewrite, unless the sandbox refuses that too.

